---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Callout from "astro-theme-university/components/Callout.astro";
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import ApologyAnatomy from "../components/ApologyAnatomy.astro";
import HomeIcons from "../components/HomeIcons.astro";
import MovementTimeline from "../components/MovementTimeline.astro";
import TwoResponses from "../components/TwoResponses.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import idrisPhoto from "../content/people/idris-fenn.png";
import marisolPhoto from "../content/people/marisol-quaye.png";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { MOVEMENTS } from "../lib/movements";
import { siteConfig } from "../site-config";

const stats = [
  { value: "12", label: "teaching weeks" },
  { value: "3", label: "assessments" },
  { value: "10", label: "sessions" },
  { value: `${MOVEMENTS.length}`, label: "movements" },
] as const;

const team = [
  { name: "Marisol Quaye", role: "Convenor", photo: marisolPhoto },
  { name: "Idris Fenn", role: "Tutor", photo: idrisPhoto },
] as const;
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A shattered surface pieced back together with gold seams, kintsugi-style, in the course's gold-and-ink palette"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <div class="course-question-wrap">
    <Callout type="info">
      <p class="course-question-eyebrow">The question this course keeps returning to</p>
      <h2 class="course-question">What makes an apology actually work?</h2>
    </Callout>
  </div>

  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <div class="stat-strip reveal">
    {
      stats.map((stat) => (
        <div class="stat-tile">
          <p class="stat-value">{stat.value}</p>
          <p class="stat-label">{stat.label}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <div class="section-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    Every week you take a real apology — public or personal, someone else's
    or your own — and put it through the same four-stage cycle. It isn't a
    formula to memorise; it's the shape of the thinking every assignment in
    this course asks for. What changes week to week isn't the cycle itself
    but the complication layered on top of it: whose apology it is, who's
    watching, who holds the power, and what's actually owed once "sorry" has
    been said.
  </p>
  <div class="info-card-grid reveal">
    <div class="info-card">
      <HomeIcons name="magnifier" class="info-card-icon" />
      <p class="info-card-eyebrow">Stage 01</p>
      <h3 class="info-card-title">Read the situation</h3>
      <p class="info-card-body">
        You start with what actually happened: who was harmed, who's
        speaking, and whether an apology is even the right response.
        Nothing gets drafted before this is settled.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="info-card">
      <HomeIcons name="blocks" class="info-card-icon" />
      <p class="info-card-eyebrow">Stage 02</p>
      <h3 class="info-card-title">Build the apology</h3>
      <p class="info-card-body">
        Acknowledgement, responsibility, explanation, and repair are
        separate moving parts, and timing decides how they land. You
        assemble a version where each piece is actually doing work.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="info-card">
      <HomeIcons name="gauge" class="info-card-icon" />
      <p class="info-card-eyebrow">Stage 03</p>
      <h3 class="info-card-title">Stress-test it</h3>
      <p class="info-card-body">
        The same wording can succeed in private and fail in public, or the
        other way round. You check it against audience, power, and
        context, and look for the phrasing that would make things worse.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="info-card">
      <HomeIcons name="loop" class="info-card-icon" />
      <p class="info-card-eyebrow">Stage 04</p>
      <h3 class="info-card-title">Revise and deliver</h3>
      <p class="info-card-body">
        Feedback exposes what the first draft missed. You revise tone,
        medium, and timing until it's something you'd actually be willing
        to send — not just something that reads well.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <h2>The anatomy of an apology</h2>
  <p>
    Weeks 2 and 3 take the four-stage cycle apart and look at what "build
    the apology" is actually made of. These four components nest inside
    each other rather than sitting in a list — pull one out and the
    others still stand, but the apology reads differently, which is
    exactly the ablation test Week 3 runs on real examples.
  </p>
  <div class="illustration-frame reveal">
    <ApologyAnatomy />
  </div>

  <div class="section-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <h2>How the semester unfolds</h2>
  <p>
    The course doesn't front-load a formula and spend twelve weeks applying
    it. Each movement complicates the central question again — first what
    an apology is, then what it's made of, then why it fails, then how
    context, power, and aftermath change everything that came before.
  </p>
  <div class="reveal">
    <MovementTimeline />
  </div>

  <div class="section-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    This isn't only for people who want to get better at apologising
    personally — it's for anyone who ends up designing, evaluating, or
    responding to apologies as part of what they do. If one of these
    sounds like you, the material has somewhere to land.
  </p>
  <div class="info-card-grid reveal">
    <div class="info-card">
      <HomeIcons name="pen" class="info-card-icon" />
      <p class="info-card-eyebrow">For writers &amp; communicators</p>
      <h3 class="info-card-title">Communicators</h3>
      <p class="info-card-body">
        If you already think about how wording and framing change what a
        message does, apology is one of the clearest cases to study — the
        stakes make every choice visible.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="info-card">
      <HomeIcons name="hexagon" class="info-card-icon" />
      <p class="info-card-eyebrow">For product &amp; service people</p>
      <h3 class="info-card-title">Designers and builders</h3>
      <p class="info-card-body">
        Products, services, and systems fail. This course is about
        exactly the moment after that — the support message, the public
        statement, the incident report — and what makes it land or
        backfire.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="info-card">
      <HomeIcons name="people" class="info-card-icon" />
      <p class="info-card-eyebrow">For team members &amp; leads</p>
      <h3 class="info-card-title">Team members and leaders</h3>
      <p class="info-card-body">
        Conflict, feedback, and responsibility come up in every team. This
        course gives you a way to think about who owes what to whom, and
        why some corrections land while others don't.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="info-card">
      <HomeIcons name="spark" class="info-card-icon" />
      <p class="info-card-eyebrow">For the curious</p>
      <h3 class="info-card-title">Curious skeptics</h3>
      <p class="info-card-body">
        You've noticed some apologies land and others make things worse,
        and you want a real account of why — the actual mechanics of what's
        said and what it does, not generic reassurance.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <h2>Same mistake, two responses</h2>
  <p>
    The difference between these isn't tone — it's structure. The left
    column is what Week 4 spends a whole session pulling apart: hedged
    acknowledgment, no ownership, no repair. The right column is why
    Weeks 2 and 3's four components hold up under the same pressure.
  </p>
  <div class="illustration-frame reveal">
    <TwoResponses />
  </div>

  <div class="section-divider" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="team-strip reveal">
    <h2 class="team-heading">Course team</h2>
    <ul class="team-list">
      {
        team.map((person) => (
          <li class="team-person">
            <Image src={person.photo} alt="" width={112} class="team-photo" />
            <span>
              <span class="team-name">{person.name}</span>
              <span class="team-role">{person.role}</span>
            </span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <a class="team-link" href={withBase("/people/")}>Full bios and contact details →</a>
  </div>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <HomeIcons name="book" class="nav-card-icon" />
      <p>The weekly ideas the course builds from, one movement at a time.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Sessions" href="/sessions/">
      <HomeIcons name="flask" class="nav-card-icon" />
      <p>Hands-on exercises where the week's ideas get tried on real cases.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <HomeIcons name="clipboard" class="nav-card-icon" />
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <HomeIcons name="people" class="nav-card-icon" />
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<script>
  const prefersReducedMotion = matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;

  if (!prefersReducedMotion) {
    document.documentElement.classList.add("js-reveal");

    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        for (const entry of entries) {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            entry.target.classList.add("is-visible");
            observer.unobserve(entry.target);
          }
        }
      },
      { threshold: 0.15 },
    );

    document.querySelectorAll(".reveal").forEach((el) => observer.observe(el));
  }
</script>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .course-question-eyebrow {
    margin-block: 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  .course-question {
    margin-block: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1.1rem + 1.6vw, 2.1rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  /* The central course question is the one idea meant to stay visible all
     semester (CLAUDE.md, Phase 1) — the generic "info" callout otherwise
     renders in a neutral warm-grey tint indistinguishable from an ordinary
     aside, and sits below the fold of "what you will do". Re-tint it with
     the brand gold, give it more room than a passing aside, and put it
     first so it reads as the course's spine rather than a footnote. */
  .course-question-wrap {
    margin-block-end: 2rem;
  }

  .course-question-wrap :global(.at-callout) {
    --at-accent: var(--at-primary);
    --at-accent-soft: color-mix(in oklab, var(--at-primary) 12%, var(--at-bg));
    padding: 1.75rem 2rem;
    border-block-start-width: 3px;
  }

  /* Static dotted-stripe motif echoing the hero art — a fixed divider, not
     an animated shimmer, between major sections. */
  .section-divider {
    height: 3px;
    margin-block: 2.5rem;
    background: repeating-linear-gradient(
      90deg,
      var(--at-divider) 0 14px,
      transparent 14px 22px
    );
  }

  .stat-strip {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 9rem), 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-block: 0.5rem 1rem;
  }

  .stat-tile {
    padding: 1.1rem 1rem;
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    text-align: center;
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .stat-value {
    margin-block: 0 0.15rem;
    font-size: 2.1rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--at-primary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums;
  }

  .stat-label {
    margin-block: 0;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .info-card-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr));
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-block: 1.25rem 2rem;
  }

  .info-card {
    padding: 1.5rem 1.75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: 1rem;
    background: linear-gradient(
      155deg,
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--at-primary) 4%, var(--at-bg)) 0%,
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--at-primary) 10%, var(--at-bg)) 100%
    );
    transition:
      transform 0.15s ease,
      box-shadow 0.15s ease;
  }

  .info-card:hover {
    transform: translateY(-3px);
    box-shadow: var(--at-shadow-sm);
  }

  /* HomeIcons.astro is a separate component, so its rendered <svg> doesn't
     carry this file's scope attribute — :global() is required to reach it,
     same as the .at-card/.at-callout overrides below. Without it these
     rules silently no-op and the icon falls back to raw intrinsic SVG
     size (huge) and inherited text colour (dark ink, not gold). */
  :global(.info-card-icon) {
    width: 1.9rem;
    height: 1.9rem;
    margin-block-end: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  .info-card-eyebrow {
    margin-block: 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .info-card-title {
    margin-block: 0 0.5rem;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  .info-card-body {
    margin-block: 0;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .illustration-frame {
    padding: 2rem;
    margin-block: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: 1.25rem;
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  :global(.nav-card-icon) {
    width: 1.7rem;
    height: 1.7rem;
    margin-block-end: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  /* Reach into the theme's own card component for a slightly more
     tasteful hover than its default (background tint + shadow only) —
     following the same :global() escape hatch already used above for
     .at-callout, since .at-card is rendered by a different component. */
  :global(.at-card) {
    transition:
      transform 0.15s ease,
      background-color 0.15s ease,
      box-shadow 0.15s ease;
  }

  :global(a.at-card:hover) {
    transform: translateY(-3px);
  }

  .team-strip {
    padding: 1.5rem 1.75rem;
    margin-block: 1.25rem 2rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: 1rem;
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .team-heading {
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .team-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .team-person {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  .team-photo {
    width: 3.5rem;
    height: 3.5rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .team-person span {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .team-name {
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .team-role {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .team-link {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* Shared one-time scroll-reveal. Visible by default (and always under
     reduced motion, since the script above never adds `js-reveal` there)
     — JS only opts elements into a hidden starting state once it has
     confirmed it's running, so nothing depends on JS to be seen. */
  .reveal {
    transition:
      opacity 0.5s ease,
      transform 0.5s ease;
  }

  :global(html.js-reveal) .reveal {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(14px);
  }

  :global(html.js-reveal) .reveal.is-visible {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
  }
</style>
